  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\u2202A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'",
